faa-8110-49 pack: content licence
=================================

This pack is derived from the four US Federal Aviation Administration (FAA)
documents listed below (S1 to S4 of the source-vetting record). Their landing-
page URLs are held in the build record dated 2026-09-25 and are not reproduced
here; the repository link policy bans source-material hosts (see
../../docs/LICENSING.md).

Source licence: Public Domain (US Government work, 17 U.S.C. 105)  (tier 1; see
../../docs/SOURCE-VETTING.md). No source-material download link is published.

All four sources are works of the United States Government prepared by FAA
staff of the Aircraft Certification Service (AIR), and are NOT subject to
copyright protection in the United States (17 U.S.C. 105). They are in the
public domain: anyone may reproduce, transform, and redistribute them for any
purpose, including commercially, without permission and without any attribution
or share-alike obligation.

Attribution to the FAA is provided as a courtesy (see the repository NOTICE),
not as a legal obligation. The FAA does not endorse this pack or its authors.

Each source below carries a vetting block: exact title, publisher office,
pinned date, status, cancellation, licence basis, what the pack reconstructs
from it, and the chapters that use it. Items marked "confirmed from PDF" were
checked against the local source-text captures on 2026-09-25.

Reconstruction: all pack content is synthesized reference notes in the authors'
own words. The documents themselves are not republished here; long verbatim
passages are never reproduced.

NOTE: This file governs the CONTENT of this pack. The repository tooling is
licensed separately under MIT (see ../../LICENSE).


THE FOUR SOURCES
=================

S1  FAA Order 8110.49A, Software Approval Guidelines
    Publisher: US DOT Federal Aviation Administration, Aircraft Certification
    Service (AIR)   National Policy   Issued: effective 2018-03-29
    Status: Active   Signed: Dr. Michael C. Romanowski, Director, Policy &
    Innovation Division, Aircraft Certification Service (confirmed from PDF)
    Cancellation: cancels and supersedes FAA Order 8110.49 Chg 2, Software
    Approval Guidelines, dated April 10, 2017 (confirmed from PDF, Ch 1 para 4).
    Chapters 5 to 16 of Chg 2 were deleted in the change to 8110.49A because
    their topics duplicate AC 20-115D and AC 00-69 material (or were removed);
    this pack reconstructs what remains.
    Basis: US Government work, 17 U.S.C. 105
    Reconstructs: the FAA software review process (certification liaison, desk
    and on-site reviews, review planning agreements, review objectives), the
    level-of-involvement determination (criteria and the three Appendix A
    worksheets), and the software conformity inspection process (software part
    and installation conformity, ASE and ASI tasks, FAA Form 8120-10).
    Used in: ch01, ch02, ch03, ch04

S2  AC 20-115D, Airborne Software Development Assurance Using EUROCAE ED-12( )
    and RTCA DO-178( )
    Publisher: US DOT Federal Aviation Administration   Advisory Circular
    Issued: 2017-07-21   Initiated by: AIR-134   Status: Active
    Cancellation: cancels AC 20-115C, Airborne Software Assurance, dated
    July 19, 2013 (confirmed from PDF, para 3).
    Basis: US Government work, 17 U.S.C. 105
    Reconstructs: which RTCA/EUROCAE documents the FAA recognizes for airborne
    software (ED-12C/DO-178C plus the DO-330 to DO-333 supplement set), the
    criteria for continuing to use ED-12B/DO-178B processes for new
    development, expected life cycle data submittal, tool qualification under
    DO-330, supplement use, field-loadable and user-modifiable software, and
    the legacy software modification flow chart. The recognized RTCA/EUROCAE
    documents are copyrighted; this pack restates only what the AC itself says.
    Used in: ch01, ch05, ch06

S3  AC 20-152A, Development Assurance for Airborne Electronic Hardware
    Publisher: US DOT Federal Aviation Administration   Advisory Circular
    Issued: 2022-10-07   Initiated by: AIR-622   Status: Active
    Cancellation: cancels AC 20-152, RTCA, Inc., Document RTCA/DO-254, Design
    Assurance Guidance for Airborne Electronic Hardware, dated June 30, 2005
    (confirmed from PDF).
    Basis: US Government work, 17 U.S.C. 105
    Reconstructs: when ED-80/DO-254 applies, the simple versus complex custom
    device classification and its assurance consequences, verification topics
    (robustness, the HDL code coverage method), tool assessment and
    qualification, previously developed hardware, commercial off-the-shelf
    (COTS) intellectual property, COTS devices, and circuit board assemblies
    (CBA). Single event effects (SEE) susceptibility is outside the AC and
    outside this pack. The recognized ED-80/DO-254 documents are copyrighted;
    this pack restates only what the AC itself says.
    Used in: ch01, ch07, ch08, ch09, ch10

S4  AC 00-69, Best Practices for Airborne Software Development Assurance Using
    EUROCAE ED-12( ) and RTCA DO-178( )
    Publisher: US DOT Federal Aviation Administration, Aircraft Certification
    Service (AIR)   Advisory Circular
    Issued: 2017-07-21   Initiated by: AIR-134   Status: Active
    Cancellation: none. No prior AC 00-69 is cancelled. Change field blank.
    Basis: US Government work, 17 U.S.C. 105
    Reconstructs: the change impact analysis best-practice contents (what a CIA
    identifies and which change classes it addresses), the clarification that
    data coupling and control coupling are distinct and both required, and the
    design-level error-handling practices (foreseeable error sources, mitigation
    in requirements, runtime protection at levels A and B). The AC states it is
    best practices, not guidance, and complementary to ED-12C/DO-178C and to
    AC 20-115D. It is not an acceptable means of compliance.
    Used in: ch01, ch06 (cross-link), ch11, ch12, ch13


EXCLUDED MATERIAL (cited by name only; no text reproduced)
=========================================================

The following standards are copyrighted and are NOT licensed to this pack. The
FAA documents recognize them; the pack names them and restates the FAA's own
recognition and clarification text only. It reproduces no standard text, table,
objective, or clause wording from any of them.

-   RTCA DO-178C, "Software Considerations in Airborne Systems and Equipment
    Certification" (2011-12-13), and its supplements DO-330 (Software Tool
    Qualification Considerations), DO-331 (Model-Based Development and
    Verification), DO-332 (Object-Oriented Technology and Related Techniques),
    and DO-333 (Formal Methods).
-   EUROCAE ED-12C, the European equivalent of DO-178C, and its supplements
    ED-215 to ED-218.
-   RTCA DO-254, "Design Assurance Guidance for Airborne Electronic Hardware"
    (2000-04-19), and its EUROCAE equivalent ED-80 (April 2000).
-   SAE ARP4754B, "Guidelines for Development of Civil Aircraft and Systems",
    and SAE ARP4761A, "Guidelines for Conducting the Safety Assessment Process
    on Civil Aircraft, Systems, and Equipment".
-   RTCA DO-248C / EUROCAE ED-94C FAQ #67, and RTCA DO-333 / EUROCAE ED-216.
    AC 00-69 names both. This pack names them and does not quote them.

Buy these from RTCA, EUROCAE, or SAE as applicable. Their designations,
titles, and editions are identified in this pack for reference only; see the
avionics-signpost pack for the full pointer table. The exclusions and the
cleared status of S1 to S4 are recorded in ../../docs/SOURCE-VETTING.md.
